𝗗𝗮𝘆 𝟱𝟲/𝟯𝟲𝟱 — 𝗗𝗲𝘃𝗢𝗽𝘀 𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗖𝗵𝗮𝗹𝗹𝗲𝗻𝗴𝗲

🔥 𝗞𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗿𝗻𝗲𝘁𝗲𝘀 𝗦𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗰𝗲 𝗶𝘀 𝗡𝗼𝘁 𝗥𝗲𝗮𝗰𝗵𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗣𝗼𝗱𝘀 — 𝗛𝗼𝘄 𝗪𝗼𝘂𝗹𝗱 𝗬𝗼𝘂 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁 𝗜𝘁?

𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

Your Kubernetes application Pods are Running and Ready, but users cannot access the application through its Service. How would you troubleshoot this issue in production?

Here’s my practical approach 👇

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟭: 𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸 𝘁𝗵𝗲 𝗦𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗰𝗲

kubectl get svc -n <namespace>
kubectl describe svc <service-name> -n <namespace>

Verify:

- Service type
- ClusterIP or external IP
- Port and targetPort
- Label selectors

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟮: 𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸 𝗘𝗻𝗱𝗽𝗼𝗶𝗻𝘁𝗦𝗹𝗶𝗰𝗲𝘀

kubectl get endpointslices -n <namespace>
kubectl get endpoints <service-name> -n <namespace>

Confirm that the Service has the expected Pod IP addresses as endpoints.

If there are no endpoints, investigate the Service selector and Pod labels.

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟯: 𝗩𝗲𝗿𝗶𝗳𝘆 𝗣𝗼𝗱 𝗟𝗮𝗯𝗲𝗹𝘀

kubectl get pods -n <namespace> --show-labels

Compare the Pod labels with the Service selector.

A mismatch means the Service cannot select the intended Pods.

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟰: 𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸 𝗣𝗼𝗿𝘁 𝗖𝗼𝗻𝗳𝗶𝗴𝘂𝗿𝗮𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻

Review the application container port and Service "targetPort".

For example:

- Application listens on port 8080.
- Service port is 80.
- Service targetPort should be 8080.

A wrong port mapping can prevent traffic from reaching the application.

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟱: 𝗧𝗲𝘀𝘁 𝗖𝗼𝗻𝗻𝗲𝗰𝘁𝗶𝘃𝗶𝘁𝘆

Test the Service from an appropriate diagnostic Pod:

kubectl run test-curl --rm -it --restart=Never \
  --image=curlimages/curl -n <namespace> \
  -- curl -v http://<service-name>:<port>

Check whether the application responds through the Service.

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟲: 𝗜𝗻𝘃𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗴𝗮𝘁𝗲 𝗡𝗲𝘁𝘄𝗼𝗿𝗸𝗶𝗻𝗴

If the configuration looks correct, investigate:

- NetworkPolicies
- CNI plugin health
- kube-proxy or the cluster's Service dataplane
- DNS resolution
- Ingress or external load balancer configuration, if applicable

💡 𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗟𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴:

A Running and Ready Pod does not guarantee that users can reach it.

Troubleshoot the complete traffic path:

Client → Service → EndpointSlice → Pod → Application Port.
